    <note x="5" y="15" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="12" width="170" markdown="true" height="46" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0"><![CDATA[<div><b>Sample size for<br>
Sampling Best Response</b></div>]]></note>
    <note x="475" y="160" backgroundDark="0" fontSize="14" width="715" markdown="true" height="25" textColorDark="-1" textColorLight="-16777216" backgroundLight="0"><![CDATA[<div>Upper panel: <b>Strategy distribution in <span style="color: #7b3294">Sampling BR</span></b> &nbsp;&nbsp; · &nbsp;&nbsp;Lower panel: <b>Strategy distribution in <span style="color: #1b7837">Exact BR</span></b></div>]]></note>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## WHAT IS IT?

SBRUG (Sampling Best Response in Undercutting Games) compares two revision rules in undercutting games: sampling best response, under which a revising agent best responds to the strategies of a few randomly observed agents, and best response to the exact population state. Its website, [luis-r-izquierdo.github.io/sbrug](https://luis-r-izquierdo.github.io/sbrug/), explains the model and runs it online.

An undercutting game is a symmetric two-player game with ordered strategies 1, ..., horizon + 1. Lower strategies are called earlier, and higher strategies later. One way to picture it: two players share an activity that can last horizon periods; strategy i stops it at period i, except strategy horizon + 1, which never stops first. Continuing together pays, but each player can gain by stopping first: against strategy i > 1, the best reply is i - 1, and against strategy 1 it is 1.

Two games are available. In the traveler's dilemma, strategy i is the claim i + 1, with claims 2, ..., horizon + 2; both travelers are paid the lower claim, and the reward is added to the lower claimant's payment and subtracted from the higher claimant's. In the finitely repeated prisoner's dilemma with horizon rounds, strategy i cooperates until round i - 1 and defects from round i on (or from the round after the opponent's first defection); strategy horizon + 1 never defects first.

## HOW IT WORKS

Each agent revises its strategy at the times of its own rate-one Poisson clock. The next revision in the population is then made by an agent chosen at random, so the model follows this sequence of revisions: each tick consists of n-of-agents revisions, which is one unit of revision time. On average, each agent revises once per tick.

A revising agent experiments with probability prob-exp = 10 ^ log10-prob-exp, choosing a strategy at random. Otherwise:

- under **sampling best response** (Sampling BR), it observes sample-size agents, drawn at random with replacement, and chooses a strategy with the highest total payoff against them;
- under **best response to exact information** (Exact BR), it chooses a strategy with the highest expected payoff against the current population state.

Ties select the smallest strategy. Both rules run at the same time, on two populations that share the revising agents, the decisions to experiment and the experimental strategies.

## HOW TO USE IT

Choose the game and its parameters, the population and the initial state, and press **setup** and **go**. The run stops at final-time, which can be as large as 5000. The game, its parameters, the horizon, the population, the initial state and final-time take effect at the next setup; sample-size, log10-prob-exp and mean-from can also be changed during a run. The slider log10-prob-exp sets the probability of experimentation on a logarithmic scale, and the monitor next to it shows prob-exp.

The view shows snapshots of the share of the population using each strategy, from dark blue (everyone) to light blue (one agent) on a logarithmic scale; white denotes zero. It has one column per tick, or at least 300 columns in runs longer than 300 ticks; when a column covers several consecutive ticks, it shows the last of them. For example, with horizon 98, runs of up to 355 ticks have one column per tick, and a run of 5000 ticks uses 355 columns of 14 or 15 ticks. Strategies grow from bottom to top. The upper panel shows Sampling BR and the lower panel Exact BR. Orange cells mark experiments with strategies above 1 in the displayed tick; experiments and short-lived changes in the other ticks of a column do not appear.

The plot records cooperation C/n at every tick under both rules: the expected number of periods before the earlier stop in a random match, divided by the horizon. The monitors show its average over all ticks from mean-from on, including those between snapshots.

## THINGS TO NOTICE

Under Sampling BR, a rare experiment with a late strategy can be decisive for an agent that observes it, and the slightly earlier reply can be observed in turn. Under Exact BR, a rare late strategy barely changes expected payoffs.

## HOW TO CITE

Luis R. Izquierdo and Segismundo S. Izquierdo (2026). SBRUG: Sampling Best Response in Undercutting Games. NetLogo model. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23734407

## CREDITS AND REFERENCES

Related paper: Luis R. Izquierdo and Segismundo S. Izquierdo. How Sampling and Experimentation Sustain Cooperation in Undercutting Games.

Copyright (C) 2026 Luis R. Izquierdo & Segismundo S. Izquierdo. GNU General Public License, version 3 or later.]]></info>
